This section catalogues a series of events ($delta vb(x)$) such that $dv((vb(x) + delta vb(x)), t)$ is in the direction of $delta vb(x)$ (positive feedback), that eventually results in the American Revolution.

The immediate and the general driver for these events are shown, along with the extent to which they create tension between the colony and the British , and how it creates unity among the colonists

#lbl(<evt:french-indian>, event(
  "French & Indian War, or The 7 Years War",
)[British troops from their empire defend English colonies from French settlers and Indians, resulting in more land for the colonists.])

#lbl(<cuz:french-indian>, cause[The growth of the French colonies and the British colonies reach the extent of resource competition and contested borders. The war between the British Empire and France around the world makes interests in North America prominent. ])

To the colonists, having a common enemy that threats their possession of land creates unity. 

This event would lead to positive impacts on the relationship between the colony and the British as the colonies may face direct military threat without intervention by the British. 

#lbl(<evt:stamp>, event(
  "Stamp Act",
)[British taxing colonies for stamps on all written papers, first tax imposed on the colonies. ])

#lbl(<cuz:stamp>, cause[Despite the British won the war, the cost is significant and dragged them heavily in debt. Since the British colonies in America acquired significantly more land as a result of this war fought mostly by the British, the British government deemed the colonies in North America obliged in paying them back. However, the colonies fiercely resist conceding economic interests. Hence, the Parliament deemed @evt:stamp to be a compromise as a way to collect tax without being too direct. ])

Common enemy (British) to their economic interests lead to unity of the colonists. For example, a secret organization is formed to resist taxation, Sons of Liberty. 

Tax levy, though justified, severely impacts the relationship between the colonies and the British. National friendship is a justification of legitimacy rather than a factor to directly optimize for. "Don't watch excitement, watch where the money flows." This may also be seen in the U.S.'s refusal in economically assisting France which directly helped their revolution against the British. 

The fact that the colonists violently destroyed loyalist press while claiming their freedom of speech and press is another illustration of the distinction of justification of ruling legitimacy and interest-driven actions in reality. However, the separation from this hypocrisy becomes extremely amplified when the the principle in the justification of ruling legitimacy contradicts too directly with practical actions, as seen in the cause of the Civil War @sec:ramp. 

#lbl(<evt:boston>, event(
  "Boston Massacre",
)[~1000 British soldiers in Boston with ~15000 hostile inhabitants. The locals tried to provoke the soldiers in an organized fashion by throwing things including rocks. Shots fired. 2 colonists are killed.])
This tragic accident is framed by the colonists as an intentional, violent "massacre" -- as local press machines can shape public opinion in the colonies much faster than the British press machines can. This advantage may weaken due to technologies that communicate at the speed of light, though censorship and preference of local/national press keep the decisively stronger position of the colonies versus the mother nation. There may be exceptions when the mother nation masters techniques of shaping public opinions remotely, such as color revolution and systematic media warfare. Hence, the mother nation must maintain a strong grip on media power in the colonies if it were to maintain its control of the colonies. Similarly, a colony desiring independence must solve the foremost media problem as a necessary condition. 

#lbl(<cuz:boston>, cause[After several failed attempts on collecting tax from the colonies with the appointed collector often tortured by the colonists, the British decides to convince the colonists with a military show of force. ])

Unity of the colonists armed, as their enemy armed. The tension exceeds elastic limit, situation will not return to before stretch even when the force is no longer applied. From here on, neither parties may retreat, Richardson's equations have divergent solutions, or one side vanishes. 

This event also shows the extent to which a non-governor may influence trends in history. Trying the British soldiers firing shots under British courts will lead to rapid escalation of the situation, as the many cases where controversial court case triggers social unrest; trying the British soldiers under colonial courts will likely lead to immediate retaliation by the British soldiers currently in the colonies and reinforcement from the British Empire. John Adams, in this case, prevented direct escalation as a result of this event by defending the soldiers in the court. His strong reputation as a revolutionary protected him from attacks by the colonists while his oratory skill defended the soldiers optimally. However, although large scale mutual retaliation is prevented in this case, shots are hangfired not misfired. Another event will soon take tension again to the breaking point. 

#lbl(<evt:tea>, event(
  "Boston Tea Party",
)[The British Parliament imposes 3 $cent$ per pound tax on importation of tea on the colonies. This act provoked some colonists to throw boxes of tea from a ship from the British in the sea for retaliation, while dressing as American Indians.])

#lbl(<cuz:lex>, cause[Another form of taxation that failed. ])

This event ignites the colonists across all the colonies to the same ground against the British; tension at breaking point. 

Moreover, attempts to reduce tension by withholding military force, as in this case the British soldiers did not patrol near the port, result in more provocative actions. 

#lbl(<evt:intolerable>, event(
  "The Intolerable Acts",
)[The British Parliament passed a series of laws to punish the colonies. The laws include 
- Boston Port Act (March 31, 1774): Closed Boston Harbor until the destroyed tea was paid for, crippling local trade and the economy.
- Massachusetts Government Act (May 20, 1774): Placed the colonial government under direct British control and banned most town meetings.
- Administration of Justice Act (May 20, 1774): Allowed royal officials accused of crimes to be tried in Great Britain or another colony.
- Quartering Act (June 2, 1774): Allowed governors to requisition unoccupied buildings to house British soldiers.])

#lbl(<cuz:lex>, cause[Response to Boston Tea Party as punishment. ])
Unanimously hurting the colonies' interests united them as one. Clearly, when sending this ultimatum like message, the British must prepare for the hostile response by the colonies that results in a direct conflict. Therefore, uniting the colonies as one is a suboptimal strategic decision. Differentiating treatments to different colonies and using their intrinsic differences shaped by their different physical environments to divide and conquer seems like the only way forward if the British would like direct control. 

To further study the effects of these trends on microscopic units, examine where two individuals are carried to by the strong undercurrents at the time. 

Thomas Hutchinson was the crown appointed governor of Massachusetts, responsible for collecting taxes from the colony and forward it to the British. He has lived in the colonies for a large proportion of his life and devotes for the benefits of the colonies. Although he was not in favor of the taxes, he still had to collect them, otherwise this analysis will be on someone else. As a result of escalation of hostility between the colonies and the British, his position grew increasingly dangerous. Ultimately, his house was attacked by mobs and he had to flee to England for the rest of his life. Both Hutchinson and John Adams are undoubtedly devoted to the colonies but had to take actions in favor of the British, but Adams became a founding father while Hutchinson fled. The most fundamental cause here is that Hutchinson directly violated economic interests of governorate while Adams at most directly irritates connections of the people died from @evt:boston. "Men sooner forget the death of their father than the loss of their patrimony." This contrast is another example where it is clear that the economic incentives is more fundamental than social causes. 

As a notable figure renowned in both the mother country and the colonies, Benjamin Franklin is empowered in many issues, though his position was endangered when the place he resides in believes he leans more to the other side. As a result, he was publicly berated in the Parliament before he finally moves to the colonies. 

#lbl(<evt:cont-congress>, event(
  "First Congressional Congress",
)[Met in Philadelphia to coordinate a unified colonial response and boycott against British taxation and the Intolerable Acts. The delegates did not seek independence at this stage, nor did they write a founding governing framework or constitution for a new nation. They did come to a consensus, that trade will be halted with the British until @evt:intolerable is annulled.])

#lbl(<cuz:cont-congress>, cause[Attempt to unite the colonies on the matter of opposing British tax. ])

Unity across colonies establish, Patrick Henry, governor of Virginia declares: "I'm no longer a Virginian. I'm an American!"

From here, it is clear that both sides fear the domino-like encroachment of the other side if no defensive actions taken. 

Demonstrates effective communication (and cheap detection of betrayal) breaks the Nash equilibrium of all trading with the British. 

#lbl(<evt:lexington>, event(
  "Lexington & Concord (1775)",
)[British commander tries to seize the colonists' ammunition store in Lexington and Concord. This decision was quickly discovered by the local militia, who defended the store. This event marks the beginning of the revolution. ])

#lbl(<cuz:lex>, cause[ British General Thomas Gage received orders to stop a potential rebellion by confiscating hidden gunpowder, cannons, and weapons stockpiled by colonists in Concord.])

Unity among the colonists peak, war begins. 